#section("III. Where They Pull Apart")

The differences are at least as important as the resemblance, and they fall along
three axes. Each is a place where the shared _shape_ of perfection-as-trajectory is
filled with incompatible content.

#subsection("Epistemic posture: postulate versus revelation")

Kant's postulates — God, freedom, immortality — are explicitly _not_ knowledge
claims. They are what practical reason must assume for morality to be coherent,
held with what Kant calls moral faith rather than theoretical certainty; the
immortality on which endless progress depends is postulated, not proved
@kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:122], and its acceptance is a need of pure practical reason, not
an insight into a supersensible fact @kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:126]. The whole critical
project is built to block exactly the kind of confident metaphysical speculation
about the soul, God, and eternity that would turn a postulate into a doctrine: the
first _Critique_ insists that "thoughts without content are empty, intuitions
without concepts are blind" @kant-cpr[A51/B75], and the bounds of sense it draws
are precisely bounds on what the understanding may claim to _know_ about the
unconditioned.

Latter-day Saint theology occupies the opposite posture. It makes confident,
revelation-based metaphysical claims about premortal existence, the structure of
the afterlife, and the nature of godhood; the King Follett discourse advances them
not as regulative assumptions but as disclosed facts @king-follett, and the Book of
Mormon frames the very reception of truth as a matter of revelation — "by the power
of the Holy Ghost ye may know the truth of all things" @book-of-mormon[Moroni 10:5].
This is not a difference of detail but of genre. Kant's whole discipline is designed
to forbid what the tradition takes itself licensed by revelation to affirm. The two
are not competing theories at the same epistemic level; they are claims held under
two different warrants, and the warrant is where the deepest divergence lies.

#subsection("Asymptote versus arrival")

For Kant, the finite rational being never actually _becomes_ holy. It approaches
holiness forever, and a qualitative gap between the creature and the holy will —
which is to say, God — remains permanently in place; the endless progress is
progress precisely because it never terminates @kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:122]. The
asymptote is the point: to close the gap would be to end the moral striving that,
for Kant, constitutes the creature's dignity.

Eternal progression in Latter-day Saint thought is more radical. The doctrine of
exaltation holds that humans can actually become gods themselves — a real future
parity of kind, compactly stated in Lorenzo Snow's couplet that as man now is, God
once was, and as God now is, man may become @snow, and grounded in the King Follett
teaching that God himself was once as man now is @king-follett. Scripture is read
in the same key: the Sermon's command to be perfect is repeated in the Book of
Mormon as an attainable end — "be perfect even as I, or your Father who is in heaven
is perfect" @book-of-mormon[3 Nephi 12:48] — and Moroni's closing exhortation
promises a real completion: those who come to Christ are "perfected in him"
@book-of-mormon[Moroni 10:32]. That is a difference of degree becoming a difference
of kind — indeed a collapse of the very gap Kant fixes permanently in place. Kant
would almost certainly see the move as overstepping the limits of practical
postulation into dogmatic metaphysics: the asymptote has been declared to arrive,
and on the strength of a revelation his critical philosophy will not admit as
knowledge.

#subsection("The kind of God at the end")

The third divergence concerns the terminus itself. Kant's God is a
non-anthropomorphic postulate, needed to guarantee that virtue and the happiness
proportioned to it eventually coincide — the highest good requires a moral author
of the world @kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:124]. This God is a moral world-governor, not a
being a human could become; the postulate secures the coherence of the moral life
without populating the supersensible with a describable person. The Latter-day
Saint God is embodied, personal, and ontologically continuous with humanity,
differing from the exalted human in degree of glory and knowledge rather than in
kind of being @king-follett — the very continuity the Johannine promise that "we
shall be like him, for we shall see him as he is" @bible[1 John 3:2] is read to
underwrite. For Kant the process ends at a God categorically unlike its
participants; for the tradition it ends at a God the participants have joined.
